        <!-- KURSAD_BORE_COLLISION_END -->
        <!--
          FUSION GEOMETRY (Payload_Capture_Body_V1). The 0.14 x 0.05 x 0.07 box
          that used to be here was a hand-entered stand-in for this CAD; the
          real part measures 142 x 52 x 70 mm, so the box was right to within
          1 mm per side. Meshes come from ~/KURSAD40/cad_exports/payload/ via
          Tools/simulation/gz/cad/update_hook_from_cad.sh and are shared with
          Tools/simulation/gz/models/kursad_payload/.

          UNITS: metres. mm->m is baked into the vertices, not applied with
          <mesh><scale>.

          POSE +0.008: the CAD frame runs z -0.043 (bottom plate underside) to
          +0.027 (deck top), i.e. its origin is 8 mm below the part's mid
          height. The link origin here is the mid height, so the mesh is
          lifted 8 mm to put CAD -0.043/+0.027 exactly on link -0.035/+0.035 -
          the same top and bottom faces the collision box still uses.

          COLOUR IS UNCHANGED, deliberately. The exact ambient/diffuse values
          below are the ones the HSV detector was tuned against (see the
          colour note on payload_blue). The bottom plate is given the same
          colour rather than a neutral one so the part reads as a single
          solid blob from every angle, exactly as the box did - the
          down-facing camera never sees it, but nothing is gained by making
          it a second colour.
        -->
        <visual name="visual">
          <pose>0 0 0.008 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><mesh><uri>model://kursad_payload/meshes/payload_body.stl</uri></mesh></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.05 0.10 0.75 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.05 0.10 0.75 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <visual name="bottom_plate_visual">
          <pose>0 0 0.008 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><mesh><uri>model://kursad_payload/meshes/payload_bottom_plate.stl</uri></mesh></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.05 0.10 0.75 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.05 0.10 0.75 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <!-- Ayni Payload_Capture_Body parcasi, ayni celik hedef diski; tam
             gerekce icin payload_red'e bakin. -->
        <visual name="target_disk_visual">
          <pose>0 0 0.0053 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.006</radius><length>0.001</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.32 0.33 0.36 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.55 0.57 0.62 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.9 0.9 0.9 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Sun -->
    <!--
      OUTDOOR LOOK, TIER 1 (2026-08-27). This is still the ONLY light in
      the world; a second shadow casting light was considered and
      rejected (cost, and a second shadow across the red triangle).

      intensity 1 to 1.1 and diffuse 0.904 neutral to 1.0 0.98 0.94.
      A warm late afternoon key instead of a studio grey one. A LIGHT
      colour is not a surface colour, so it can never enter an HSV mask
      by itself; what it does is bias the per channel gain. The red
      channel gets gain 1.0 while blue gets 0.94, which pushes red
      surfaces slightly warmer and slightly more saturated. That is the
      direction of travel we want, because the red triangle has only a
      1.44x area margin against HSV_MIN_AREA_TRI_BASE 390 and is the
      first thing to fail in poor light.

      Checked against the real marker materials rather than assumed:
        red_triangle   ambient/diffuse 1 0 0. Diffuse term
          0.780 (N dot L) * 1.1 * (1,0,0)*(1.0,0.98,0.94) = (0.858,0,0),
          plus ambient 0.5*(1,0,0) = (0.5,0,0). Sum clamps to (255,0,0),
          i.e. H 0, S 255, V 255. Dead centre of red band 1. Unchanged
          from before in direct sun (it already clamped); the gain is in
          shade, which is what the ambient lift is for.
        blue_hexagon   ambient 0.04 0.10 0.28, diffuse 0.00 0.55 1.00,
          emissive 0.000 0.004 0.541. Summing diffuse + ambient +
          emissive and clamping gives roughly (5,132,255), i.e. H 105,
          S 250, V 255. Before this change it computed to about H 107.
          Both sit mid band in [90,140] with 15 units of headroom to the
          lower edge, so the warm tint costs nothing.

      specular 0.271 to 0.20, LOWERED ON PURPOSE, and this is the part
      that must not be "fixed" later. blue_hexagon's material carries
      <specular>0.9 0.9 0.9 1</specular>; it is intentionally glossy.
      A specular highlight is near white, i.e. S near 0, so raising the
      sun's specular blows out the hexagon's saturation and pushes it
      toward failing HSV_COLOR_FRAC_HEX 0.45. Every brightening above is
      therefore paid for with a specular cut: 0.9*0.271 = 0.244 becomes
      0.9*0.20 = 0.18, a 26% smaller white lobe on the glossiest surface
      in the arena.

      <direction> IS BYTE IDENTICAL AND MUST STAY THAT WAY. All shadow
      geometry in the arena, and every mission run the HSV thresholds
      were tuned against, assumes the sun sits exactly where it sits.
    -->
    <light name="sunUTC" type="directional">
      <pose>0 0 500 0 0 0</pose>
      <cast_shadows>true</cast_shadows>
      <intensity>1.1</intensity>
      <direction>0.001 0.625 -0.78</direction>
      <diffuse>1.0 0.98 0.94 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.20 0.20 0.20 1</specular>
      <attenuation>
        <range>2000</range>
        <linear>0</linear>
        <constant>1</constant>
        <quadratic>0</quadratic>
      </attenuation>
      <spot>
        <inner_angle>0</inner_angle>
        <outer_angle>0</outer_angle>
        <falloff>0</falloff>
      </spot>
    </light>

    <!-- Geo coords (PX4 default) -->
    <spherical_coordinates>
      <surface_model>EARTH_WGS84</surface_model>
      <world_frame_orientation>ENU</world_frame_orientation>
      <latitude_deg>47.397971057728974</latitude_deg>
      <longitude_deg>8.546163739800146</longitude_deg>
      <elevation>0</elevation>
    </spherical_coordinates>




</world>
</sdf>
